# Is an efficient society a good society?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why can a deeply unequal outcome be perfectly efficient? How do we choose among efficient outcomes?"
next: {"title":"Can markets deliver any fair outcome we want?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/27-can-markets-deliver-any-fair-outcome-we.cast"}

## Same output, different shares
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Belgium
      - Denmark
      - Sweden
      - Germany
      - United States
    values:
      - 25
      - 28
      - 29
      - 31
      - 40
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 50
    y_label: Gini after taxes and transfers (0–100)
    colors:
      - green
      - green
      - green
      - green
      - red
    label_size: 22
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 510
    bar_colors: same
sources:
    - id: oecd_idd
      title: 'OECD Income Distribution Database (via Our World in Data: Gini coefficient before and after tax)'
      authors: OECD
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Gini of disposable income 2022: Belgium 0.250, Denmark 0.276, Sweden 0.290, Germany 0.309, United States 0.396; market income: US 0.512, France 0.524, Germany 0.505, Belgium 0.481, Finland 0.512 (disposable 0.274).'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/inequality-of-incomes-before-and-after-taxes-and-transfers-scatter
    - id: pwt
      title: 'Penn World Table 11.0 (via Our World in Data: output per hour worked)'
      authors: Feenstra, Inklaar and Timmer
      year: 2025
      finding: 'GDP per hour worked 2022, int-$ 2021 prices: Sweden 81.1, US 81.9, Germany 82.7, Belgium 83.7, Denmark 90.0.'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/labor-productivity-per-hour-pennworldtable

    card title "Is an efficient society a good society?"

Is an efficient society a good society? Start with five rich countries that work about equally productively.
    draw axes

B: Belgium, Denmark, Sweden, Germany and the United States. In 2022 each made between eighty-one and ninety dollars of output per hour worked.
    text prod "each makes\n$81–90\nper hour worked\n(2022)" cites ["pwt"] x 820 y 480 font_size 32

Now, how evenly does each share what it makes? These bars are the Gini coefficient after taxes and transfers: zero if everyone had the same income, a hundred if one household had it all.
    highlight axes

B: Belg (@draw bar_1@)ium twenty-five, Denm (@draw bar_2@)ark twenty-eight, Sw (@draw bar_3@)eden twenty-nine, Ger (@draw bar_4@)many thirty-one.
    blocking false

    ask "Drag the United States bar to where you think its Gini is." on bar_5 store g default "28" voice b
        intro: Where would you put the United States?
        right: Forty. You said {g}: close.
        wrong: Forty. You said {g}.

Forty. As productive per hour as Germany, and far more unequal than Belgium.
    highlight bar_5 bar_4

B: So which of them is efficient?
    highlight bar_1 bar_5

Efficiency in the economist's sense means no waste left: nobody can gain without someone else losing. That test never asks about shares.
    highlight prod

An honest caveat: output per hour is only a rough proxy for efficiency. A country can be productive per hour and still waste plenty.
    text cav "rough proxy\nfor efficiency" #8f887c x 820 y 300 font_size 28

    erase cav

B: Still, the picture holds: similar productivity, very different shares. Many points, all near the same frontier.
    text many "many points,\none frontier" #2f6b8f x 820 y 300 font_size 36

    pause 0.5

## Who picks the point?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - United States
      - France
      - Germany
      - Belgium
    values:
      - [51, 52, 50, 48]
      - [40, 30, 31, 25]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 60
    y_label: Gini (0–100)
    bar_colors: same
    colors:
      - red
      - green
      - green
      - green
    label_size: 22
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: oecd_idd
      title: 'OECD Income Distribution Database (via Our World in Data: Gini coefficient before and after tax)'
      authors: OECD
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Gini of disposable income 2022: Belgium 0.250, Denmark 0.276, Sweden 0.290, Germany 0.309, United States 0.396; market income: US 0.512, France 0.524, Germany 0.505, Belgium 0.481, Finland 0.512 (disposable 0.274).'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/inequality-of-incomes-before-and-after-taxes-and-transfers-scatter

    card title "Who picks the point?"

B: So the shares differ. Where does the difference come from? Look at incomes before the state steps in.
    draw axes

Market income: wages, profits and rents, before any tax is paid or any benefit received.
    text t_before "before taxes\nand transfers" #3d3833 cites ["oecd_idd"] x 820 y 520 font_size 34

B: My guess: America is already far more unequal in the market.
    pause 0.3

    quiz "Before taxes and transfers, how unequal is the US compared with France, Germany and Belgium?"
        right: About the same: before taxes and transfers, all four sit near fifty.
        wrong: The big gap appears later, not in the market: here, about the same.
        * Far more unequal
        + About the same
        * Far less unequal

Abou (@draw bar_1@)t the same. The US fif (@draw bar_2@)ty-one, France fifty-t (@draw bar_3@)wo, Germany fifty (@draw bar_4@), Belgium forty-eight.
    blocking false

    erase t_before

B: Now add taxes and transfers: pensions, child benefits, unemployment pay, income tax.
    text t_after "after taxes\nand transfers" #2f6b8f cites ["oecd_idd"] x 820 y 520 font_size 34

The US comes down to forty. France and Germany to about thirty. Belgium to twenty-five.
    animate stage 1 duration 3 ghost true

B: So the market's split looked alike. The difference came afterwards, through taxes and benefits that voters and parliaments chose.
    highlight bar_1 bar_4

Try it: slide between before and after, and watch which countries move most.
    explore params ["stage"]

    animate stage 1 duration 0.5

That's who picks the point. Not the market: politics, and the institutions it builds.
    text econ "the frontier:\neconomics" #2f6b8f x 820 y 370 font_size 32

B: So the frontier is economics. Where to stand on it is ethics, settled by politics.
    text eth "the point:\nethics, settled\nby politics" #b5482e x 820 y 240 font_size 32

    pause 0.5

## Why choose differently?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Europe
      - United States
    values:
      - 26
      - 60
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 80
    y_label: '% saying the poor are lazy'
    bar_colors: same
    colors:
      - green
      - red
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 150
      w: 520
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: ags2001
      title: Why Doesn't the US Have a European-Style Welfare State?
      authors: Alberto Alesina, Edward Glaeser and Bruce Sacerdote
      year: 2001
      finding: 'World Values Survey: 60% of Americans vs 26% of Europeans say the poor are lazy; the authors conclude racial heterogeneity and American political institutions explain most of the US–Europe difference in redistribution.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w8524
    - id: okun1975
      title: 'Equality and Efficiency: The Big Tradeoff'
      authors: Arthur Okun
      year: 1975
      finding: 'Redistribution as carrying money in a leaky bucket: some is lost on the way.'
    - id: oecd_idd
      title: 'OECD Income Distribution Database (via Our World in Data: Gini coefficient before and after tax)'
      authors: OECD
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Gini of disposable income 2022: Belgium 0.250, Denmark 0.276, Sweden 0.290, Germany 0.309, United States 0.396; market income: US 0.512, France 0.524, Germany 0.505, Belgium 0.481, Finland 0.512 (disposable 0.274).'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/inequality-of-incomes-before-and-after-taxes-and-transfers-scatter

    text wvs "World Values\nSurvey, 1990s" #8f887c cites ["ags2001"] x 800 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c_bel "beliefs?" #3d3833 x 800 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_race "race,\ninstitutions?" #b5482e cites ["ags2001"] x 800 y 360 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_okun "Okun 1975:\na leaky bucket" #8f887c cites ["okun1975"] x 800 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s1 "Efficiency can't\npick the point:\nconsensus" #2f6b8f x 800 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s2 "Why different points,\ncost of moving:\ncontested" #b5482e x 800 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait okun x 800 y 330 width 180 of "Arthur Okun" cameo true hidden true
    scratch fin cites ["oecd_idd"] work ["Gini, 2022: before → after taxes","United States   51 → 40","Finland   51 → 27"] x 500 y 440 font_size 34 hidden true
    scratch party work ["US-style shares → Belgian-style","same output per hour?"] x 500 y 440 font_size 34 hidden true
    scratch recap work ["similar output per hour","very different shares","the point: chosen by politics"] x 500 y 420 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Why choose differently?"

If politics picks the point, why do similar countries pick such different ones?
    draw axes

B: Alberto Alesina, Edward Glaeser and Bruce Sacerdote asked exactly that in 2001. One clue sat in the World Values Survey of the 1990s.
    draw wvs

Asked whether the poor are lazy, twenty-six percent of Europeans said yes.
    draw bar_1

    quiz "In the same survey, what share of Americans said the poor are lazy?" voice b
        right: Sixty percent: more than twice the European share.
        wrong: Americans were far more likely to blame effort than luck: here, sixty percent.
        * 26%
        * 40%
        + 60%
        * 80%

B: Sixty percent of Americans did. If poverty looks like (@draw c_bel@) a choice, helping looks less fair.
    draw bar_2

But their own data pointed harder at race and institutions: racial division made welfare unpopular wi (@highlight bar_2@)th many American voters, and American political institutions limited the political power of the poor.
    draw c_race

B: So is it values or institutions? Still argued, and the two shape each other.
    highlight c_bel c_race

    erase c_bel c_race wvs

And moving between points may itself cost some output. Arthur Okun, in 1975, called redistribution a leaky bucket; how leaky it is comes later in the course.
    draw c_okun okun

    erase c_okun okun

B: How sure are we? That efficiency alone can't pick the point: consensus.
    draw s1

Why countries pick different points, and how much moving between them costs: contested.
    draw s2

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 s1 s2

    draw fin

    quiz "In 2022 Finland's Gini before taxes and transfers was 51, the same as America's. After them it was 27. What best explains the gap?"
        right: Taxes and transfers: the market left both equally unequal, and Finland's politics moved it.
        wrong: Before taxes the two were the same, so look at what came after: here, taxes and transfers.
        * More productive workers
        + Taxes and transfers Finland chose
        * Freer markets

    erase fin

    draw party

    quiz "A party promises to move a country from US-style to Belgian-style shares with no loss of output. What does that promise most need evidence for?" voice b
        right: That the leak is small: the promise is that redistribution costs no output.
        wrong: Which claim could data check, and which is a value judgement? Here: that the leak is small.
        * That the new point is efficient
        + That the leak is small
        * That Belgium is fairer

    erase party

So is an efficient society a good society? Efficiency can bring a country to the frontier, but rich countries with almost the same productivity stand at v (@draw recap_line_3@)ery different places on it.
    draw recap_box recap_line_1 recap_line_2

B: Where to stand is a choice a society makes, and keeps making. Whether a market can reach any point it chooses is the next question.
    highlight recap_line_3

    pause 0.5

